> human to a Turing machine, and arguing that a human is not a turing
> machine by using a diagonalization method.  I pretty clearly
> remember finding some crucial flaws in that "proof".  Details can
> be provided to those interested.  The rest of the book wasn't

I'm interested. Thanks. I'm highly suspect of proofs anyway, even if
they're airtight. I think Godel has pretty well opened the can on proofs,
made them subject to other issues, such as rather than proving why
something can't be done, devising ways that it can be. In my original
post, I mentioned genetic programming, which seems to be a way around
Penrose's proof.

In principle, the only thing Turing machines lack that humans have is the
ability to define classes (sets) and to operate on sets and make sets of
sets. Add this piece of ability (thru genprog?) and you're there.

'course, most of the HUMANS I know with this ability aren't that
intelligent anyway; isn't the real question how to make robots think BETTER?




