From: Mr John M Casey <jmcasey@HK.Super.NET>
Date: Mon, 16 May 1994 12:41:25 +0800 (HKT)
Subject: [#94-5-198] ECON: The limits to comparative advantage

On Thu, 12 May 1994, Ray wrote:

> What happens
> if GP_1 holds stock A and GP_2 wants to buy stock A, and both
> GP_1 and GP_2 predict a nice rise in the price of A? Well, if I
> was GP_1, my decision would be to hold stock A, and thus the
> value of the stock would jump to the predicted price. Thus, GP_2's
> predictive ability doesn't help at all because as soon as
> GP_2 processes the information neccessary to predict the price rise,
> the market has already pushed the price up and the opportunity is
> lost (assuming near equitable distribution of information)
> The market may not be that efficient now, but imagine what will
> happen when everyone has genetic programming brokers. The bounty will
> go to those with the most computing resources and the fastest and
> most complete information. (e.g. the elite)

Okay, sure, except that I'd bet that emergent properties would give people
a way of profiting on this that the GP's can't forecast. Actually, it's
probably a meta-learning process that stays a step ahead of the programs.
I know this sounds like a self-contradiction, since the faster the GPs can
process the more efficient the pricing mechanism SHOULD become, but isn't
the goal of GP (at least in this case) to think like people only faster?
Suppose you have a million GPs working the market, all targetting stock A,
currently at price X and headed for fundamental reasons to price Y. Since
feedback isn't in fact instantaneous (it's faster, probably faster than we
humans can process, but NOT instantaneous), machines just like humans
probably would overbid the market price to, say price Z, at which price
the stock becomes a sell.

Actually, I'm a quant. And actually, I'm not so sure there really is a
single price above which a stock or a market is overvalued. The ultimate
uncertainty is a function of that ultimately unquantifiable and ultimately
emergent process known as sentiment. And I believe that GPs will be no
better than people at quantifying and acting on changes in sentiment. What
SHOULD happen is that the process will massively speed up, so that at
higher resolution you end up with the same chart, but at "human
resolution" the chart takes on similar, fractal characteristics. Which
would mean no essential improvement in trading capability.




